WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.514 --> 00:00:03.085
- [Narrator] An eight-ton,
45-foot sperm whale carcass

00:00:03.085 --> 00:00:04.312
washes up on your beach.

00:00:04.312 --> 00:00:05.630
What do you do? You can't move it.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:06.771
- [Paul] The thing is falling apart.

00:00:06.771 --> 00:00:07.654
- [Narrator] You can't bury it.

00:00:07.654 --> 00:00:09.007
- [Paul] There's water under the sand.

00:00:09.007 --> 00:00:10.436
- [Narrator] You can't
throw it back in the ocean.

00:00:10.436 --> 00:00:12.233
- [Paul] Of course, the tides can push them back in.

00:00:12.233 --> 00:00:15.400
- [Narrator] Might as well blow it up?

00:00:20.781 --> 00:00:22.826
It was an ordinary day in November, 1970.

00:00:22.826 --> 00:00:25.968
- [Paul] November 12, 1970, to be exact.

00:00:25.968 --> 00:00:26.973
- [Narrator] Thanks, Paul.

00:00:26.973 --> 00:00:28.286
This here is Paul Linnman.

00:00:28.286 --> 00:00:31.061
He was a television reporter
for Portland's KATU,

00:00:31.061 --> 00:00:33.198
and on November 12, 1970,

00:00:33.198 --> 00:00:34.120
Paul was assigned a story

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:35.935
that would change his life forever.

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:39.506
- What to do with one
45-foot, eight-ton whale,

00:00:39.506 --> 00:00:42.650
dead on arrival on a beach near Florence.

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:44.341
Whales washing up on the beach in Oregon

00:00:44.341 --> 00:00:46.366
are not an uncommon occurrence.

00:00:46.366 --> 00:00:47.694
- [Narrator] But it had been so long,

00:00:47.694 --> 00:00:48.950
state officials didn't
know how to get rid of

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:50.733
that giant, stinking carcass.

00:00:50.733 --> 00:00:52.243
- [Paul] That's precisely why

00:00:52.243 --> 00:00:53.434
the Oregon Department of Transportation

00:00:53.434 --> 00:00:54.900
decided to use dynamite.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:55.815
- [Narrator] The plan
was to blow the whale

00:00:55.815 --> 00:00:56.789
into smaller pieces,

00:00:56.789 --> 00:01:00.714
and have scavengers like
seagulls eat the rest of it.

00:01:00.714 --> 00:01:02.152
Paul and his cameraman set up their shot

00:01:02.152 --> 00:01:05.652
and waited a thousand yards away from the blast.

00:01:06.842 --> 00:01:10.384
- [Paul] Within, I'd say,
about 20 or 30 seconds,

00:01:10.384 --> 00:01:13.366
it was soon raining down on all of us.

00:01:13.366 --> 00:01:14.841
And larger pieces that came down,

00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:16.496
it became louder and louder

00:01:16.496 --> 00:01:20.737
until you realized, wow, if
one of these chunks hits me,

00:01:20.737 --> 00:01:21.737
I'm a goner.

00:01:22.942 --> 00:01:25.335
We start running in the
direction of the parking lot,

00:01:25.335 --> 00:01:26.683
and that's when a piece of blubber

00:01:26.683 --> 00:01:29.298
about the size of a small coffee table

00:01:29.298 --> 00:01:32.215
completely flattened an Oldsmobile.

00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:36.571
- [Narrator] KATU's exploding whale report

00:01:36.571 --> 00:01:39.422
was a viral video in the pre-internet age.

00:01:39.422 --> 00:01:41.894
It's inspired parodies and one-man shows,

00:01:41.894 --> 00:01:45.451
and for Paul Linnman,
it's been career-defining.

00:01:45.451 --> 00:01:47.849
- [Paul] It's something
that reoccurs in my life

00:01:47.849 --> 00:01:51.637
in one way or another every single day.

00:01:51.637 --> 00:01:55.804
I know what's gonna be
written about in my obituary.

